-### Parameters
-*   **Slope parameter $m$:** Influences the range of action of the truncated gradient in the image plane. A lower $m$ yields a near-constant long-range gradient approximating the cut-off gradient; a higher $m$ yields a sharp decay from the cut-off gradient [S1].
-*   **Pruning threshold $\tau_{pruning}$:** Opacity threshold below which a Gaussian is considered "dead" and eligible for truncated gradients [S1].
-*   **Tile-coverage radius threshold:** Radius below which a dead Gaussian's tile coverage is expanded [S1].
-
-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Model Divergence:** Accumulated truncated gradients may surpass the true gradient, especially for "very bad fits" (no normal gradients) or "good fits" (true gradient near zero) [S1]. This is prevented by applying the truncated gradient **only** for dead Gaussians ($\sigma_i < \tau_{pruning}$) [S1].
-*   **Repulsion:** Applying the truncated gradient when $\frac{\partial L}{\partial \alpha_i} \ge 0$ causes a "long-range push," making Gaussians repel each other [S1].
-*   **Performance Overhead:** Without careful implementation, the method can be 1–10× slower to train than the baseline [S1]. This is mitigated by applying radius expansion and truncated gradient computation only for dead Gaussians and using motion masks for dynamic scenes [S1].
-*   **Tile Culling:** Dead Gaussians may be culled by the rasterizer before they can receive gradients. Expanding their tile-coverage radius addresses this [S1].
+**Parameters:**
+*   **Slope parameter $m$:** Influences the range of action of the truncated gradient in the image plane. A lower $m$ yields a near-constant long-range gradient approximating the cut-off gradient; a higher $m$ yields a sharp decay from the cut-off gradient [S1]. Typical values are not given in the sources.
+*   **Pruning threshold $\tau_{pruning}$:** Used to identify 'dead' Gaussians. Only applied to Gaussians with $\sigma_i < \tau_{pruning}$ to prevent divergence [S1].
+*   **Rasterization Cutoff:** Implicitly $1/255$ for the $\sigma\alpha$ product check [S1].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Model Divergence:** Accumulated truncated gradient may surpass the true gradient, especially for 'very bad fits' (no normal gradients) or 'good fits' (true gradient near zero). This is prevented by applying the truncated gradient ONLY for dead Gaussians ($\sigma_i < \tau_{pruning}$) [S1].
+*   **Repulsion:** If the alpha gradient is positive, applying the truncated gradient causes a long-range push, making Gaussians repel each other. The sign check in Step 1 prevents this [S1].
+*   **Tile Culling:** Dead Gaussians may be culled by the rasterizer's tile-based culling. Expanding the tile-coverage radius for dead Gaussians addresses this [S1].
+*   **Performance Overhead:** The method can be 1–10× slower to train than the baseline. Optimizations include restricting radius expansion and truncated gradient computation to dead Gaussians and using motion masks for dynamic scenes [S1].
 
